00:01:08.298 --> 00:01:10.977
Originally the owner
started as an American diner

00:01:10.977 --> 00:01:13.199
and a Chinese owner
purchased the business,

00:01:13.199 --> 00:01:14.720
so he incorporated Chinese food in it.

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:15.969
You can get pancakes,
or yeah, unconventional

00:01:15.969 --> 00:01:17.633
with the chow mein, it just works.

00:01:17.633 --> 00:01:19.115
- This diner is my
childhood 'cause that's what

00:01:19.115 --> 00:01:20.112
I would eat growing up.

00:01:20.112 --> 00:01:21.482
- We're gonna serve you
guys the Uncle John's

00:01:21.482 --> 00:01:22.315
steak and eggs.

00:01:22.315 --> 00:01:23.586
We start off with a cut of New York steak.

00:01:23.586 --> 00:01:24.640
It's about eight ounces.

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:26.808
We marinate it with a
little bit of soy sauce,

00:01:26.808 --> 00:01:28.345
garlic salt, salt and pepper.

00:01:28.345 --> 00:01:29.243
- And it's $11?

00:01:29.243 --> 00:01:32.050
- $11, and it's not only the
steak, it comes with two eggs

00:01:32.050 --> 00:01:34.400
however you like it and it also
comes with a side and toast.

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:36.001
- How can you provide it at this price?

00:01:36.001 --> 00:01:38.417
- We have other items on
the menu that helps outweigh

00:01:38.417 --> 00:01:39.578
the cost of the steak.

00:01:39.578 --> 00:01:40.891
For a diner, you need the steak and eggs

00:01:40.891 --> 00:01:42.851
and it's just on the menu
because people want steak

00:01:42.851 --> 00:01:44.010
and eggs for breakfast.

00:01:44.010 --> 00:01:47.546
- Not just $11 steak, $11
steak plus fried eggs,

00:01:47.546 --> 00:01:48.693
plus pancakes.

00:01:48.693 --> 00:01:50.044
- And they have Thai tea here.

00:01:50.044 --> 00:01:50.904
- [Andrew] And black coffee.

00:01:50.904 --> 00:01:51.737
- Cheers.

00:01:51.737 --> 00:01:52.824
- This is probably the
most excited I've ever been

00:01:52.824 --> 00:01:54.760
for a meal in my life.

00:01:54.760 --> 00:01:56.345
- That is so good.

00:01:56.345 --> 00:01:57.178
Whoa.

00:01:58.190 --> 00:02:00.646
- Yeah, dog, she knew what she was doing.

00:02:00.646 --> 00:02:01.959
- We have to cheers the steak.

00:02:01.959 --> 00:02:06.126
- How about we like elbow
touch, like we just touch forks.

00:02:08.509 --> 00:02:09.342
(groans in pleasure)

00:02:09.342 --> 00:02:10.175
Yeah, that's good.

00:02:10.175 --> 00:02:11.279
Look at that delicious morsel.

00:02:11.279 --> 00:02:12.698
- My mom used to cook
steaks for me growing up.

00:02:12.698 --> 00:02:15.309
This is exactly the same
recipe she would use.

00:02:15.309 --> 00:02:16.189
Love you, Mama Lim.

00:02:16.189 --> 00:02:17.022
- Let's get real here.

00:02:17.022 --> 00:02:18.417
This is America on a plate.

00:02:18.417 --> 00:02:21.055
Fried rice, two eggs, a giant ass steak.

00:02:21.055 --> 00:02:24.253
- Ketchup, syrup, chill
oil, salt and pepper,

00:02:24.253 --> 00:02:26.133
A1 steak sauce, Sriracha.

00:02:26.133 --> 00:02:27.776
- This is seriously one of
the best meals I've ever had

00:02:27.776 --> 00:02:28.609
in my life.

00:02:28.609 --> 00:02:29.893
I can tell you right now, this wins.

00:02:29.893 --> 00:02:32.490
- You're not gonna find a
better deal on steak anywhere.

00:02:32.490 --> 00:02:34.198
It's okay, man.

00:02:34.198 --> 00:02:36.273
- Let's go to the next place, I guess.

00:02:36.273 --> 00:02:37.190
- Let's go.

00:02:44.068 --> 00:02:44.972
- Wow.

00:02:44.972 --> 00:02:46.215
- Yeah, that steak was really good.

00:02:46.215 --> 00:02:47.673
(phone rings)

00:02:47.673 --> 00:02:50.645
- Hey, Mom, how's it going?

00:02:50.645 --> 00:02:54.812
- So we ate steak and on the
side was fried rice and eggs.

00:02:55.855 --> 00:02:57.348
(laughing)

00:02:57.348 --> 00:03:01.382
- It was really good, I'm
here with Andrew and Adam too.

00:03:01.382 --> 00:03:02.632
- [Steven] Yes.

00:03:04.615 --> 00:03:06.156
- All right, love you too, bye.

00:03:06.156 --> 00:03:07.645
- I'm ready for a medium rare steak fact.

00:03:07.645 --> 00:03:08.529
- Steak fact.

00:03:08.529 --> 00:03:11.273
Did you know that you're
more likely to die from a cow

00:03:11.273 --> 00:03:12.477
than a shark?

00:03:12.477 --> 00:03:14.375
- Describe a cow related death.

00:03:14.375 --> 00:03:16.517
- You know, sometimes cows
get tired of you milking it

00:03:16.517 --> 00:03:17.867
and they just revolt.

00:03:17.867 --> 00:03:18.911
- What's our band name?

00:03:18.911 --> 00:03:20.431
- How do we feel about
the Three Buzzkateers.

00:03:20.431 --> 00:03:22.216
That was a fan favorite.
- Mmm.

00:03:22.216 --> 00:03:23.049
- It'll grow on you.

00:03:23.049 --> 00:03:26.088
- Nothing grows on me except hair.

00:03:26.088 --> 00:03:27.461
(crickets chirping)

00:03:27.461 --> 00:03:28.798
Probably some moles.

00:03:28.798 --> 00:03:31.769
(easygoing music)

00:03:31.769 --> 00:03:33.903
- My name is Andreas Roller,
I'm the executive chef here

00:03:33.903 --> 00:03:36.251
at Nick &amp; Stef's
Steakhouse in downtown L.A.

00:03:36.251 --> 00:03:39.564
Here we have our beautiful
28 day dry aged prime

00:03:39.564 --> 00:03:40.599
bone in rib eye.

00:03:40.599 --> 00:03:42.814
We dry age here on premises.

00:03:42.814 --> 00:03:44.067
- Why do you dry age?

00:03:44.067 --> 00:03:45.401
- Maybe not the right
term but it's a little bit

00:03:45.401 --> 00:03:47.751
like spoiling on a very controlled level

00:03:47.751 --> 00:03:49.371
to tenderize the meat.

00:03:49.371 --> 00:03:51.463
This little fur here
might look a little scary.

00:03:51.463 --> 00:03:52.985
This piece, we cut all this off.

00:03:52.985 --> 00:03:55.030
This is an indication
that a dry aging process

00:03:55.030 --> 00:03:56.332
has happened correctly.

00:03:56.332 --> 00:03:58.784
Another thing that happens
is kinda like when you reduce

00:03:58.784 --> 00:04:02.265
a sauce, if there is less
moisture, flavors get intensified.

00:04:02.265 --> 00:04:03.889
- This is not dry aged yet.

00:04:03.889 --> 00:04:05.632
- [Andreas] This is not dry
aged, this arrived yesterday.

00:04:05.632 --> 00:04:08.342
It's still pretty moist
and this has been 28 days

00:04:08.342 --> 00:04:09.226
in the dry age.

00:04:09.226 --> 00:04:10.501
This is the real deal.

00:04:10.501 --> 00:04:13.127
Here you see I put a lot
of salt and a lot of people

00:04:13.127 --> 00:04:14.766
when they first see this,
they're like, oh my god,

00:04:14.766 --> 00:04:17.391
this is a lot of salt, but
believe me at 28 ounces,

00:04:17.391 --> 00:04:19.667
you wanna make sure your
steak is well seasoned.

00:04:19.667 --> 00:04:20.985
So I'm putting our steaks on.

00:04:20.985 --> 00:04:22.567
What we're looking for here is to get

00:04:22.567 --> 00:04:24.684
that caramelized crust on the outside.

00:04:24.684 --> 00:04:27.483
The characteristic of the
flavor, I always describe it as,

00:04:27.483 --> 00:04:29.554
it has a bit nuttiness,
you know when you have

00:04:29.554 --> 00:04:31.107
a cheese that is matured.

00:04:31.107 --> 00:04:31.940
- It's like a good

00:04:31.940 --> 00:04:32.871
stinkiness.
- Deeper flavors, yes.

00:04:32.871 --> 00:04:34.033
- Yeah.

00:04:34.033 --> 00:04:35.008
Andrew.
- Steven.

00:04:35.008 --> 00:04:37.066
- This is the greatest
presentation we've ever received

00:04:37.066 --> 00:04:37.976
on this show.

00:04:37.976 --> 00:04:39.776
- I'm actually very confused
because this is only

00:04:39.776 --> 00:04:41.169
our second stop of the day.

00:04:41.169 --> 00:04:43.404
- [Steven] Oh my goodness!

00:04:43.404 --> 00:04:44.237
Do you see that?

00:04:44.237 --> 00:04:45.632
- My knife has never had an easier time

00:04:45.632 --> 00:04:46.885
going through meat.

00:04:46.885 --> 00:04:47.718
- Cheers.

00:04:54.279 --> 00:04:55.323
(sighs in pleasure)

00:04:55.323 --> 00:04:56.367
Wow.

00:04:56.367 --> 00:04:57.410
- Yeah, holy moly.

00:04:57.410 --> 00:04:58.728
- This is how people live.

00:04:58.728 --> 00:04:59.715
(laughing)

00:04:59.715 --> 00:05:01.964
- It's like a satisfying savory taffy.

00:05:01.964 --> 00:05:03.980
Perfectly tender, you
gotta chew it for a second

00:05:03.980 --> 00:05:05.807
and then it just
dissolves into your mouth.

00:05:05.807 --> 00:05:07.271
- Sometimes when you get things this big,

00:05:07.271 --> 00:05:08.155
you get tired of them.

00:05:08.155 --> 00:05:09.430
- No.
- It doesn't happen here.

00:05:09.430 --> 00:05:10.965
- I'm also gonna say
that eating this steak

00:05:10.965 --> 00:05:12.953
with this big, bowing
knife really does a lot

00:05:12.953 --> 00:05:15.036
for the whole experience.

00:05:16.156 --> 00:05:16.989
That was way good.

00:05:16.989 --> 00:05:18.824
- We have one more steak
to eat, are you ready?

00:05:18.824 --> 00:05:21.491
- This is my burden to bear now.

00:05:25.232 --> 00:05:27.266
- That steak was magnificent.

00:05:27.266 --> 00:05:29.960
- I definitely went into
that steak situation

00:05:29.960 --> 00:05:32.586
thinking I know what steaks
are gonna taste like.

00:05:32.586 --> 00:05:34.673
(beat boxing)

00:05:34.673 --> 00:05:35.590
Steak fact.

00:05:36.597 --> 00:05:37.430
Steak fact.

00:05:37.430 --> 00:05:38.491
- This is the best fact we've ever had.

00:05:38.491 --> 00:05:40.717
In a drought, beef prices rise.

00:05:40.717 --> 00:05:43.297
(playful music)

00:05:43.297 --> 00:05:44.457
- That's it?
- That's it.

00:05:44.457 --> 00:05:46.432
- In a drought, the price
of everything rises.

00:05:46.432 --> 00:05:47.265
- Not true.
- It's a drought.

00:05:47.265 --> 00:05:48.098
- Hot air.

00:05:49.295 --> 00:05:50.951
We're in Beverly Hills, baby.

00:05:50.951 --> 00:05:55.626
How can this steak be more
worth than the last two?

00:05:55.626 --> 00:05:58.923
- I guess like come from a fancier cow.

00:05:58.923 --> 00:06:01.756
(classical music)

00:06:07.391 --> 00:06:11.425
- I am Hilary, the chef de
cuisine at Cut, Beverly Hills.

00:06:11.425 --> 00:06:13.294
Today I'm grilling two steaks.

00:06:13.294 --> 00:06:15.297
Grilling a dry aged New York strip steak.

00:06:15.297 --> 00:06:16.762
It's dried for 35 days.

00:06:16.762 --> 00:06:18.891
The dry age is $48.

00:06:18.891 --> 00:06:19.821
- [Steven] That's actually
a pretty good deal.

00:06:19.821 --> 00:06:20.656
- [Hilary] It's a great deal.

00:06:20.656 --> 00:06:23.571
The other steak is a
Japanese New York strip.

00:06:23.571 --> 00:06:25.082
$25 an ounce.

00:06:25.082 --> 00:06:28.125
- So what separates
this $25 an ounce steak?

00:06:28.125 --> 00:06:30.764
- This Japanese beef is
from Miyazaki Prefecture.

00:06:30.764 --> 00:06:33.188
These cattle have been bred
for thousands of years.

00:06:33.188 --> 00:06:35.393
I have a certificate that
tells me all about this family.

00:06:35.393 --> 00:06:36.698
You can trace it back 80 years.

00:06:36.698 --> 00:06:38.411
- So who are we eating today?

00:06:38.411 --> 00:06:39.598
- I don't wanna give this a name,

00:06:39.598 --> 00:06:40.641
we're about to eat the food.

00:06:40.641 --> 00:06:41.845
- Yeah, but it's a beautiful product,

00:06:41.845 --> 00:06:42.960
so we should respect it.

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:45.124
- Exactly, this is the
reason that cow was raised,

00:06:45.124 --> 00:06:46.281
for you to enjoy it.

00:06:46.281 --> 00:06:47.905
- We're gonna be eating Kango today.

00:06:47.905 --> 00:06:50.064
- Order in a steak, it gets
seasoned with our house

00:06:50.064 --> 00:06:51.937
steak salt, it's quite a few ingredients

00:06:51.937 --> 00:06:55.115
and then it's grilled over
white oak and charcoal

00:06:55.115 --> 00:06:56.954
and then the steak comes off, it rests

00:06:56.954 --> 00:06:59.824
and we flash it in our
1,200 degree griller,

00:06:59.824 --> 00:07:02.058
brush it with a little
garlic butter, fleur de sel

00:07:02.058 --> 00:07:03.494
and it's to your table.

00:07:03.494 --> 00:07:04.626
- [Steven] So this whole
process takes about how long?

00:07:04.626 --> 00:07:07.555
Is it an eyeball thing,
is it you're counting?

00:07:07.555 --> 00:07:09.525
- [Hilary] No, you feel it,
after you do it for so long,

00:07:09.525 --> 00:07:10.358
you feel it.

00:07:10.358 --> 00:07:12.336
- Is there a better cut
of steak in the world?

00:07:12.336 --> 00:07:14.138
- If there is, I haven't had it yet.

00:07:14.138 --> 00:07:16.646
(classical music)

00:07:16.646 --> 00:07:18.412
(glasses clinking)

00:07:18.412 --> 00:07:19.895
- Did you hear that?

00:07:19.895 --> 00:07:20.728
- [Steven] That's fresh.

00:07:20.728 --> 00:07:22.517
- Definitely feel the breeze
off the ocean on that one.

00:07:22.517 --> 00:07:23.910
- [Steven] It's a nice little appetizer.

00:07:23.910 --> 00:07:26.301
Chef Hilary wanted us to first try her $48

00:07:26.301 --> 00:07:28.045
dry aged prime New York strip.

00:07:28.045 --> 00:07:30.218
- [Andrew] Like a hot
knife through butter.

00:07:30.218 --> 00:07:31.718
- [Steven] Cheers.

00:07:34.887 --> 00:07:35.786
- [Andrew] Very interesting.

00:07:35.786 --> 00:07:37.339
- That was a wonderfully tender steak.

00:07:37.339 --> 00:07:38.367
It was a nice dance.

00:07:38.367 --> 00:07:40.200
- Do your steak dance.

00:07:42.311 --> 00:07:43.195
All right, stop that.

00:07:43.195 --> 00:07:44.531
- [Steven] And now the grand finale,

00:07:44.531 --> 00:07:46.459
Japanese beef from Miyazaki.

00:07:46.459 --> 00:07:48.201
- My nose is having almost as much fun

00:07:48.201 --> 00:07:49.822
as my mouth is on this one.

00:07:49.822 --> 00:07:52.667
Let me select us some
delightful morsels to eat.

00:07:52.667 --> 00:07:54.481
- [Steven] My heart is racing right now.

00:07:54.481 --> 00:07:56.757
- [Andrew] Okay that was
the most fun I've ever had

00:07:56.757 --> 00:07:59.497
cutting something before.

00:07:59.497 --> 00:08:00.330
- Cheers.

00:08:04.118 --> 00:08:06.368
(laughing)

00:08:13.679 --> 00:08:15.762
Mm, okay, that's the one.

00:08:16.880 --> 00:08:19.492
- That is a completely new thing.

00:08:19.492 --> 00:08:22.304
- There's fat marbled into
every single bite that you get.

00:08:22.304 --> 00:08:24.669
It tastes like pure luxury.

00:08:24.669 --> 00:08:26.916
- I'm like embarrassed
to be watched eating this

00:08:26.916 --> 00:08:29.496
because it's like something so pleasurable

00:08:29.496 --> 00:08:30.658
shouldn't be done in public.

00:08:30.658 --> 00:08:32.021
Do we let camera guy have some?

00:08:32.021 --> 00:08:33.521
- Yeah, we should.

00:08:38.170 --> 00:08:39.677
That's the first time I've
seen Adam really smile

00:08:39.677 --> 00:08:41.229
before in my life.

00:08:41.229 --> 00:08:42.273
- Was this worth it?

00:08:42.273 --> 00:08:43.779
- 1000% yes.

00:08:43.779 --> 00:08:44.941
- So you're gonna come back here.

00:08:44.941 --> 00:08:46.536
- Yes, I'm gonna bring
my girlfriend back here.

00:08:46.536 --> 00:08:48.767
If you were to use one
final word to describe

00:08:48.767 --> 00:08:51.463
this entire experience,
what would that word be?

00:08:51.463 --> 00:08:52.597
- Legendary.

00:08:52.597 --> 00:08:55.336
Seriously though, candy corn?

00:08:55.336 --> 00:08:56.169
- Fine.

00:09:00.875 --> 00:09:02.773
Which steak was the most
worth it at its price?

00:09:02.773 --> 00:09:05.497
No restaurant has understood who I am

00:09:05.497 --> 00:09:07.630
as a human being more than Uncle John's.

00:09:07.630 --> 00:09:09.327
- I think Uncle John's is the only one

00:09:09.327 --> 00:09:12.690
that you cannot argue
that it isn't worth it.

00:09:12.690 --> 00:09:14.707
- This is the hardest
decision I've ever had to make

00:09:14.707 --> 00:09:15.540
on this show.

00:09:15.540 --> 00:09:17.840
I can't believe I'm saying this, Cut wins.

00:09:17.840 --> 00:09:20.044
You can't pay to have your
life changed like that.

00:09:20.044 --> 00:09:21.640
- I think I choose Nick &amp; Stef.

00:09:21.640 --> 00:09:23.575
There was a lot of
different things happening

